        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-da79265af287b715\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Victoria’s written “jeg har elsket Dem” (“I have loved you”) is an explicit retrospective avowal presented as true and intensified by “loved only you throughout my entire life.” It does not itself undertake a future commitment. Although written under extreme emotion near death, the letter is deliberate and repeatedly resumed, so the evidence supports passionate truth-conditional avowal rather than an independently reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration helps Victoria seek final emotional proximity to Johannes, but that contextual purpose does not give the core love avowal undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Victoria writes the declaration to Johannes in a farewell letter composed as she is dying.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Victoria finally discloses a lifelong love that she says she had previously been unable to show. The impending final separation, her desire to feel near Johannes, her request for forgiveness, and the invocation of God as witness heighten the avowal’s solemnity and credibility. Her explicit reflection on why she is writing and her repeated returns to the letter indicate purposeful confession, not an unmediated verbal reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ja, Johannes, jeg har elsket Dem, elsket bare Dem i hele mit liv.”\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical declaration and its expansion explicitly present Victoria’s lifelong, exclusive love as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Det er Victoria som skriver dette og Gud læser det over min skulder.”\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the utterance as written and frames it as a solemn avowal witnessed by God.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier in the letter Victoria explains that, now that she is dying, she is writing once more to tell Johannes how she has loved him, and says she has returned to writing the letter many times that day.\",\"supports\":\"Shows deliberate, reflective disclosure rather than reflex-like production; emotional intensity alone therefore does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward she bids Johannes farewell forever and asks his forgiveness.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding act is a final confession and leave-taking, not a promise or relational undertaking enacted by the core love declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is near the end of Knut Hamsun’s Victoria and is tagged as an affirmative perfect form.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates the retrospective form and terminal placement of the written avowal without determining its force by itself.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core as a retrospective avowal of a loving state. The letter’s emotional intensity and farewell function are adequately handled as context; no independent P, E, or residual O dimension is required.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
